<header>
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="eyebrow">Case Study · AI-Orchestrated Research Platform</span>
    <h1 class="hero">I let AI write the code. I owned <i>every decision that made it trustworthy.</i></h1>
    <p class="hero-sub"><b>EventFlow</b> is a research platform that scans a ~1,550-name equity universe every night, unattended, and answers one question honestly: which setups deserve attention, and where does the evidence stop. Claude Code wrote most of the implementation. The value sat in specifying, reviewing, gating, and releasing it — the requirements, architecture, validation, controls, and release calls were mine. That division of labor is the whole point of the project.</p>
    <div class="meta-row">
      <div class="m"><div class="k">Role</div><div class="v">Owner: requirements → release</div></div>
      <div class="m"><div class="k">AI tooling</div><div class="v">Claude Code (implementation)</div></div>
      <div class="m"><div class="k">Stack</div><div class="v">Python · 416 tests</div></div>
      <div class="m"><div class="k">Status</div><div class="v">Research only · nothing trades</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<section>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <p>Over about nine weeks I built EventFlow: a five-stage chained pipeline that runs after the close with no supervision, then grades itself. The engineering was largely AI-authored. What made it a research instrument rather than a pile of scripts was governance: preregistered hypotheses locked before any test ran, acceptance criteria on every output, a self-verifying health check, and a review discipline strict enough to catch bugs that every unit test had waved through. <b>That is almost exactly the posture a regulated analytics team wants around AI</b> — a capable model doing the work, and a human owning the requirements, the controls, and the decision to ship.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">01 · WHO OWNED WHAT</span><h2>AI implemented. I governed.</h2><p class="sub">The interesting part of an AI-built system is the boundary. Here is exactly where it sat.</p></div>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="col ai">
        <h3>Claude Code — implementation</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Wrote most of the pipeline, detectors, and data-access code from my specs</li>
          <li>Drafted test scaffolding and refactors on request</li>
          <li>Produced first-pass implementations I then reviewed line by line</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="col me">
        <h3>Me — requirements &amp; control</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Requirements &amp; architecture:</b> the five stages, interfaces, and what "correct" meant for each</li>
          <li><b>Validation:</b> the preregistration protocol and the statistical gates every candidate had to clear</li>
          <li><b>Review:</b> multi-round correctness review of AI output, including adversarial fixtures</li>
          <li><b>Controls &amp; release:</b> fail-loud health checks, and the call on what shipped, what was demoted, and what was killed</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">02 · THE PIPELINE</span><h2>Five stages, then it grades itself.</h2><p class="sub">Offline-first and scheduled nightly. The final health check asks whether each output actually landed fresh, not merely whether the job exited zero.</p></div>
    <div class="pipe">
      <div class="pnode"><span class="sn">1</span><b>Broker auth</b></div><div class="parrow">→</div>
      <div class="pnode"><span class="sn">2</span>W / M / D pattern scan → <b>A+ shortlist</b></div><div class="parrow">→</div>
      <div class="pnode"><span class="sn">3</span>Geometry-review batch</div><div class="parrow">→</div>
      <div class="pnode"><span class="sn">4</span>Universe OI sweep · <b>~375k contracts/day</b></div><div class="parrow">→</div>
      <div class="pnode"><span class="sn">5</span>Flow-story step</div>
    </div>
    <div class="healthcheck"><b>Self-verifying health check →</b> grades the run on freshness and coverage, not exit codes. A stage that silently returned stale data is a <b>FAILED</b> run, not a green one.</div>
    <div class="prose"><p>Price geometry and the backtest run offline from a decades-deep cache; the live broker feed is used only to re-verify the short list and pull option chains. Daily-timeframe patterns are scanned for context but <b>structurally barred</b> from the validated shortlist — they inform, they never qualify.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">03 · THINGS I BUILT INTO IT</span><h2>Instruments that refuse to flatter the operator.</h2></div>
    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="cell"><div class="idx">i.</div><h3>Unusual-OI lab</h3><p>Screens positions in <b>dollars, not contracts</b>. That single reframing killed a whole class of false positives that raw contract counts had been surfacing.</p></div>
      <div class="cell"><div class="idx">ii.</div><h3>Flow-story engine</h3><p>Resolves yesterday's option volume into <b>opened, closed, or churned</b> by differencing overnight-settled open interest, and measures premium pressure beyond what delta explains.</p></div>
      <div class="cell"><div class="idx">iii.</div><h3>Drift detection</h3><p>Names the real fault — an <b>IP-allowlist rejection</b> — instead of blaming the token, so a stale feed can't masquerade as a healthy one. Paired with a NEW badge and an HTML dashboard tab.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">04 · THE JUDGMENT, NOT THE PATTERNS</span><h2>When AI wrote the code, review is the job.</h2><p class="sub">A model that passes its own tests is not the same as a correct system. Here is where owning the review mattered.</p></div>
    <div class="calls">

      <div class="call">
        <div class="tagline">Review caught what tests missed</div>
        <h3>Eight bugs the suite passed straight through — including a <i>reversed put-direction sign.</i></h3>
        <p>One three-round correctness review of the AI-written code returned eight bugs. The worst: a put-direction sign was reversed, and every test passed anyway because the fixture contained only calls. No amount of green CI would have found it; a human reading the logic against adversarial cases did. That is exactly why the suite now stands at <b>416 tests</b> and why call/put fixtures are no longer allowed to be one-sided.</p>
        <div class="receipt">3-round review &rarr; 8 correctness bugs<br><span class="dim">worst:</span> put-direction sign reversed &middot; <span class="dim">tests passed (calls-only fixture)</span><br>suite: <span class="dim">&rarr;</span> 416 tests</div>
      </div>

      <div class="call">
        <div class="tagline">Preregistration</div>
        <h3>I locked the hypotheses <i>before</i> I could see the answers.</h3>
        <p>The research spine is preregistration: hypotheses and decision rules are written and <b>SHA-256 locked</b> before any test runs, so I can't quietly move the goalposts to fit a result. The gates have rejected every candidate so far. That is a feature, not a failure — the discipline is what makes a "no edge" verdict believable.</p>
        <div class="receipt">preregister &rarr; SHA-256 lock &rarr; then test<br><span class="dim">candidates promoted to date:</span> 0</div>
      </div>

      <div class="call">
        <div class="tagline">Killed my own darling</div>
        <h3>The most promising rule cleared the numbers and <i>still didn't ship.</i></h3>
        <p>A frozen <b>1,488-symbol study over 371,882 formations and 1.49 million simulations</b> returned no tradable standalone edge. The best candidate, a volume-sequence pattern, posted <b>+0.80pp yearly benchmark-relative with a 95% interval excluding zero</b> — and still failed the sign-consistency and multiple-testing gate. I logged it as a lead, not a signal. Passing one test is not clearing the bar I set.</p>
        <table class="evtable">
          <tr><th>Candidate</th><th>Edge</th><th>95% interval</th><th>Verdict</th></tr>
          <tr><td>vol-seq</td><td>+0.80pp/yr</td><td>excludes 0</td><td>lead, not promoted</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p class="evcap">Frozen 1,488-symbol study · 371,882 formations · 1.49M simulations. Failed sign-consistency + multiple-testing gate despite a clean headline.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="call">
        <div class="tagline">Un-foolable by design</div>
        <h3>The ledger <i>can't peek at the future,</i> and it caught 848 impossible trades.</h3>
        <p>Every simulated decision is recorded append-only, with the date the data was known kept strictly separate from the date it resolved, so nothing leaks backward. That discipline surfaced a bug: 848 simulated trades that could never have been taken, because a stop had been placed on the wrong side of the entry. I root-caused it, fixed it, and cleaned the forward record before it accumulated another day of noise.</p>
        <div class="receipt">leakage guard<br>data_as_of &ne; event_at &middot; append-only &middot; walk-forward<br><span class="dim">caught</span> 848 un-takeable sims <span class="dim">&rarr; root-caused &amp; fixed</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="call">
        <div class="tagline">Fail loud</div>
        <h3>It breaks <i>in the open,</i> never in silence.</h3>
        <p>A research tool that quietly degrades is worse than one that stops. So a run that can't read its data reports FAILED, not COMPLETE. The broker connection is probed with a hard timeout, so an expired token can never hang the pipeline. And the corporate-event calendar, once its dates go stale, returns UNKNOWN (unverified) rather than a confident all-clear it hasn't earned.</p>
        <div class="receipt">fail-loud<br>coverage gate &middot; bounded token probe &middot; catalyst UNKNOWN &ne; NONE</div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="sec-num">05 · STATUS, STATED PLAINLY</span><h2>The edge is not proven. The instrument is.</h2></div>
    <div class="callout">
      <p>No profit is claimed and none exists yet. The instrument is early into accumulating the forward outcomes that will confirm or kill the backtest, and that clock is measured in months, not days.</p>
      <p>What <b>is</b> finished is the instrument itself: honest, leakage-proof, and built to tell me the truth even when the truth is "we don't know yet." AI wrote most of it; I own why it can be trusted. <b>416 tests pass.</b></p>
      <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--ink-dim)">Research only · no orders, positions, or account surface anywhere in the codebase.</p>
    </div>
